#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186249828 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d4e178c7-bdb4-5139-a20c-3e17de51ee11" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Distance Between Two Points With Variable Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e40a6195-e0a6-5569-962b-194638c1b2ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249830} {:answer/id #uuid "6789bb35-fc2a-529d-89ea-8706539aeccc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249831} {:answer/id #uuid "0e71e43a-0165-58ba-861e-1e7ac75f1539" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pm 6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249832} {:answer/id #uuid "64137b70-d0e1-5f89-afa3-14159ebf7acc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pm 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249833} {:answer/id #uuid "6b70beb1-4d50-5923-a65b-f63e167fe831" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pm 9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249834}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6b70beb1-4d50-5923-a65b-f63e167fe831" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pm 9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249834} :answer-field/id #uuid "c2a37dcf-9eee-593f-b167-d4ee34dd3a0a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249829}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3329b56b-ec4b-5fc6-8723-d1077c57a901" :question/math-academy-id "q-118944" :question/problem "Consider the points $A(-3s,-4s,7s)$ and $B(s,-6s,3s).$ Given that the distance between $A$ and $B$ is $54,$ find the possible values of $s.$" :question/worked-solution "The distance $d$ between two points $A({x}_{1},{y}_{1},{z}_{1})$ and $B({x}_{2},{y}_{2},{z}_{2})$ is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\sqrt{({x}_{2}-{x}_{1})^{2}+({y}_{2}-{y}_{1})^{2}+({z}_{2}-{z}_{1})^{2}}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have $A{{(-3s,-4s,7s)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{1}\\text{, }{y}_{1}\\text{, }{z}_{1}\\text{)}}$ and $B{{(s,-6s,3s)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{2}\\text{, }{y}_{2}\\text{, }{z}_{2}\\text{)}}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{(s-(-3s))^{2}+(-6s-(-4s))^{2}+(3s-7s)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(4s)^{2}+(-2s)^{2}+(-4s)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16{s}^{2}+4{s}^{2}+16{s}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36{s}^{2}} \\\\  & =6\\,|s|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $d=54,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6\\,|s| & =54 \\\\ |s| & =9 \\\\ s & =\\pm 9.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083449 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b5f9ee9-a489-5048-8346-0116a19e9b4a" :knowledge-point/title "Counting the Number of Possible Rational Roots of a Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b0b34f77-b71e-574a-ad30-148d70662b1e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080025} {:answer/id #uuid "dccb6d16-40ab-5941-b6ef-ebcb81e681fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080026} {:answer/id #uuid "55c235f6-2e2e-580a-aeaf-a1ac639046ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080027} {:answer/id #uuid "61605504-7726-57b9-9c30-c820c20a4ccd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080028} {:answer/id #uuid "71958dc6-d901-574e-b7ed-d93aaa62b45e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080029}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b0b34f77-b71e-574a-ad30-148d70662b1e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080025} :answer-field/id #uuid "9f9fb03d-5750-5b30-83b0-438bef68a5b7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080024}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "df06ddf2-698f-54af-b10e-2e279114c1c7" :question/math-academy-id "q-14147" :question/problem "For the polynomial $f(x) = 2x^{3} - 7x^{2} + 7x - 2$, how many *potential* roots are given by the rational roots theorem?" :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial:\n\n- The constant term is $-2,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1$ and $\\pm 2.$\n\n- The leading coefficient is $2,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1$ and $\\pm 2.$\n\nFirst, we list out all the possibilities for the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm \\frac{1}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{2}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we reduce the fractions to lowest terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm 1, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, \\\\ \\pm 2, & \\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that some potential roots are duplicates (shown above in red). Removing these duplicates, and listing those remaining in order from least to greatest in absolute value, we have\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{1}{2},\\,\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 2.\n$$\n\nThis makes a total of $6$ potential roots. (Remember that each $\\pm $ sign accounts for $2$ roots, and $2\\cdot 3=6.$)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186249835 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5d4a21cd-bd8d-5572-b932-76875827170a" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Terms of a Fibonacci Sequence Given in Sequence Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "db112308-76e2-59ee-a782-d3af4c5c73c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249837}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "db112308-76e2-59ee-a782-d3af4c5c73c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249837} :answer-field/id #uuid "f80d8a3c-fc64-51cd-b4fe-2ae93b210522" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249836}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9fb3c079-a60e-5bda-bfec-324752a909a3" :question/math-academy-id "q-237438" :question/problem "If ${a}_{n+2}={a}_{n+1}+{a}_{n}$ with ${a}_{1}=0$ and ${a}_{2}=3,$ then ${a}_{3}=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The recursive formula is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+2}={a}_{n+1}+{a}_{n}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=1$ into the recursive formula gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1+2} & ={a}_{1+1}+{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{3} & ={a}_{2}+{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{3} & =3+0 \\\\ {a}_{3} & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186249838 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dcedd9d0-f87c-58b2-9ef1-c9fa0ef2e5c1" :knowledge-point/title "Determining an Unknown Value Given the Inverse of a Rational Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c95ceacd-c9bc-5cec-b342-df5855bba0cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249840}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c95ceacd-c9bc-5cec-b342-df5855bba0cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249840} :answer-field/id #uuid "548003a4-06f8-530b-a264-d0ff9736ac62" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249839}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fb65edc8-082c-52ff-844e-1ac02a93d381" :question/math-academy-id "q-276793" :question/problem "The function $f(x)=\\frac{5}{kx-1}$ has an inverse function given by ${f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{5+x}{x}.$ What is the value of $k?$\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{5}{kx-1}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{5}{ky-1}.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{5}{ky-1} \\\\ x(ky-1) & =5 \\\\ kxy-x & =5 \\\\ kxy & =5+x \\\\ y & =\\frac{5+x}{kx}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{5+x}{kx}.\n$$\n\nComparing our answer with the given expression, we conclude that $k=1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186249841 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "03456ac4-5798-56cc-8c14-0a645b2e232e" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Sum of the Roots of a Quadratic Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "74be56fe-6024-5e32-bb17-f6bb442cdfa9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249843} {:answer/id #uuid "15a875f2-d2d5-5c06-9198-8435fb0d4aff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249844} {:answer/id #uuid "2e6ca8d9-e8dd-57be-846f-25ff22488b22" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{8}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249845} {:answer/id #uuid "12fa5be9-1ba2-562a-b62c-04e01dad2d90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{7}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249846} {:answer/id #uuid "84d1f0c9-4e7e-5f5c-90bd-dbf09292e16a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249847}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2e6ca8d9-e8dd-57be-846f-25ff22488b22" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{8}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249845} :answer-field/id #uuid "11504366-ff1e-5f73-a407-cb0484718acb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249842}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "70cecb06-4a85-561a-ae02-e92e4d923c8d" :question/math-academy-id "q-288779" :question/problem "Consider the quadratic equation\n\n$$\n3{x}^{2}+8x-1=0.\n$$\n\n**Without** solving the equation, determine the sum of its solutions." :question/worked-solution "Suppose the quadratic equation\n\n$$\na{x}^{2}+bx+c=0\n$$\n\nhas the solutions $x={x}_{1}$ and $x={x}_{2},$ where $a\\ne 0.$ \n\nThen, Vieta's formulas state the following:\n\n- The sum of the solutions is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-\\frac{b}{a}.\n$$\n\n- The product of the solutions is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=\\frac{c}{a}.\n$$\n\nFor the given quadratic equation, we have\n\n$$\na=3,\\,b=8,\\,c=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sum of the solutions is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{b}{a} & =-\\frac{8}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186249848 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "833ac63c-235b-5059-ab7c-56eb1e44d79e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Argument of a Complex Number in the Second Quadrant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cdcf49bb-f7e2-5c23-9333-af5c047c56cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.60" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249850}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cdcf49bb-f7e2-5c23-9333-af5c047c56cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.60" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249850} :answer-field/id #uuid "7ac20aed-4025-5c62-9c23-22fcb072ca0f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249849}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "18ca621b-aa9c-527c-a411-a69f8c48899c" :question/math-academy-id "q-319492" :question/problem "Find, **in radians**, the argument of $z=-5+3\\text{i}.$ Round your final answer to **two decimal places**.\n\n**Hint**: The argument of a complex number is in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$\n\n$\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z)≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Let's plot $z$ in the complex plane.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13939908/assets/217fc7ba4c6a6c18d0be184a9d5cc7b0a682ed2bd0da6e284955040643bc2e03.png)\n\nThe complex number $z$ lies in the second quadrant of the complex plane. Hence $\\frac{\\pi }{2}<\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z)<\\pi .$\n\nIf $\\alpha $ is the angle that $z$ makes with the negative real axis, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\alpha  & =\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ \\alpha  & =arctan\\,(\\frac{3}{5}) \\\\  & =0.540\\,419…\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}⁡(z)=\\pi -0.540\\,419…≈2.60,\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060189 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "993c8fe4-dae9-5dc4-9f94-5012f905e0ee" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Correct Quadrant for Negative Angles that Contain More Than One Full Rotation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a5c8c555-d55b-5e0d-aa75-b737a5241000" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "IV" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058118} {:answer/id #uuid "b7f25f06-fe90-5802-aa3d-0112b3a684b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Between quadrants III and IV" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058119} {:answer/id #uuid "f2ed5c0e-5cd1-5c21-8ab4-7b17715efc0b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058120} {:answer/id #uuid "0a296985-8836-588f-a91b-c454533df752" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058121} {:answer/id #uuid "34b94185-8dea-5e9f-a07d-7a2ad1aa7052" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058122}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b7f25f06-fe90-5802-aa3d-0112b3a684b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Between quadrants III and IV" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058119} :answer-field/id #uuid "1593d830-9542-5875-964e-e7c5607bdfbb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186058117}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8483a20c-9e8e-5588-94ee-dd0e0014806e" :question/math-academy-id "q-34325" :question/problem "The angle $θ =-450^{∘}$ is represented in the coordinate plane in the usual way. In which quadrant does it lie?" :question/worked-solution "First, we find an angle that's coterminal with $-{450}^{∘}$ by *adding* integer multiples of ${360}^{∘}$ until we get an angle that lies in the range $[{0}^{∘},{360}^{∘}).$\n\n- $-{450}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}=-90$\n\n- $-{450}^{∘}+2\\cdot {360}^{∘}={270}^{∘}\\,✓$\n\nSo the angle ${270}^{∘}$ is coterminal with $-{450}^{∘}.$ Let's draw this coterminal angle in the coordinate plane.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13939908/assets/35b9783c9ac87b0a8d177e7bdf340b48513146404b7c768a8532da59b6ec7948.png)\n\nWe see that ${270}^{∘}$ lies between quadrants III and IV. Therefore, $-{450}^{∘}$ also lies between these quadrants."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069200 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f7614a05-f221-5fda-b491-6893f44a1a5f" :knowledge-point/title "Dividing Rational Expressions with Multiple Common Factors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a94b5953-7825-5461-91f4-945709fedb8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4a^{2}}{7bc}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066732} {:answer/id #uuid "bf48e3e8-fa3c-5e5a-a294-ba78a33ef0f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7bc}{4a^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066733} {:answer/id #uuid "fdcd6798-9338-5b10-8bce-682779f25a31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7bc}{4a^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066734} {:answer/id #uuid "128d42e5-2256-545d-8cab-23a97b2ea1a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7c^{2}}{4a^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066735} {:answer/id #uuid "3d85d47a-ebd0-5276-9805-9b5420f32656" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{7bc}{4a^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066736}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fdcd6798-9338-5b10-8bce-682779f25a31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7bc}{4a^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066734} :answer-field/id #uuid "e752e2cd-cffa-532a-9bdb-b55688ce7f48" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066731}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "452b3a54-8a97-5cfc-8253-05f85217902c" :question/math-academy-id "q-3526" :question/problem "What is $\\frac{2ab^{2}}{bc^{3}} \\div \\frac{8a^{3}}{7c^{4}}$?" :question/worked-solution "To compute\n\n$$\n\\frac{2a{b}^{2}}{b{c}^{3}}÷\\frac{8{a}^{3}}{7{c}^{4}},\n$$\n\nwe just need to multiply by the reciprocal of the divisor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2a{b}^{2}}{b{c}^{3}}÷\\frac{8{a}^{3}}{7{c}^{4}} & = \\\\ \\frac{2a{b}^{2}}{b{c}^{3}}\\cdot \\frac{7{c}^{4}}{8{a}^{3}} & = \\\\ \\frac{2ab\\cdot b}{b{c}^{3}}\\cdot \\frac{7c\\cdot {c}^{3}}{4\\cdot 2a\\cdot {a}^{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{2a}b\\cdot \\cancel{b}}{\\cancel{b}\\cancel{{c}^{3}}}\\cdot \\frac{7c\\cdot \\cancel{{c}^{3}}}{4\\cdot \\cancel{2a}\\cdot {a}^{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{7bc}{4{a}^{2}} & \\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
